1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,640
 Welcome to this lesson on telemetry and data privacy settings.

2
00:00:03,640 --> 00:00:07,080
 In this lesson, we'll focus on how data is collected

3
00:00:07,080 --> 00:00:09,640
 when you use AI-assisted development tools

4
00:00:09,640 --> 00:00:13,120
 and what control you have over that process.

5
00:00:13,120 --> 00:00:16,160
 These topics matter because they affect how your code,

6
00:00:16,160 --> 00:00:18,560
 prompts, and usage patterns are handled.

7
00:00:18,560 --> 00:00:21,920
 Telemetry refers to background data that is collected

8
00:00:21,920 --> 00:00:23,480
 while you use the platform.

9
00:00:23,480 --> 00:00:26,360
 This data helps the product team understand

10
00:00:26,360 --> 00:00:29,160
 how developers interact with different features.

11
00:00:29,160 --> 00:00:32,920
 For example, it can show which tools are used most often

12
00:00:32,920 --> 00:00:35,920
 and where users may run into issues.

13
00:00:35,920 --> 00:00:39,080
 Telemetry also helps improve system reliability

14
00:00:39,080 --> 00:00:41,360
 and performance over time.

15
00:00:41,360 --> 00:00:44,880
 Another role it plays is helping refine AI responses

16
00:00:44,880 --> 00:00:47,560
 so suggestions become more accurate.

17
00:00:47,560 --> 00:00:51,040
 Importantly, telemetry focuses on usage patterns

18
00:00:51,040 --> 00:00:53,280
 rather than personal identity.

19
00:00:53,280 --> 00:00:56,880
 Think of it as feedback generated automatically by normal use.

20
00:00:56,880 --> 00:00:59,320
 There are several categories of data

21
00:00:59,320 --> 00:01:01,880
 that may be collected through telemetry.

22
00:01:01,880 --> 00:01:05,240
 Usage patterns track which features you rely on

23
00:01:05,240 --> 00:01:08,040
 and how you move through the platform.

24
00:01:08,040 --> 00:01:10,800
 In some situations, limited code context

25
00:01:10,800 --> 00:01:14,280
 may be used to improve the relevance of AI suggestions.

26
00:01:14,280 --> 00:01:16,480
 The system can also collect AI prompts,

27
00:01:16,480 --> 00:01:20,520
 meaning the questions or requests you send to the assistant.

28
00:01:20,520 --> 00:01:23,160
 Performance metrics measure things like response time

29
00:01:23,160 --> 00:01:24,360
 and error rates.

30
00:01:24,360 --> 00:01:27,560
 Together, this data helps identify what's working well

31
00:01:27,560 --> 00:01:29,360
 and what needs improvement.

32
00:01:29,360 --> 00:01:30,880
 The goal is to improve the tool

33
00:01:30,880 --> 00:01:33,080
 not to monitor individual developers.

34
00:01:33,080 --> 00:01:36,600
 Telemetry plays a key role in improving the platform for everyone.

35
00:01:36,600 --> 00:01:38,920
 It helps teams detect bugs more quickly

36
00:01:38,920 --> 00:01:41,720
 and understand where performance can be improved.

37
00:01:41,720 --> 00:01:44,600
 By seeing common workflows, developers can design features

38
00:01:44,600 --> 00:01:46,520
 that better match real world use.

39
00:01:46,520 --> 00:01:48,880
 Telemetry also supports training AI models

40
00:01:48,880 --> 00:01:51,360
 to deliver more helpful code suggestions.

41
00:01:51,360 --> 00:01:53,280
 Another benefit is guiding decisions

42
00:01:53,280 --> 00:01:56,000
 about which new features should be built first.

43
00:01:56,000 --> 00:01:57,800
 When combined across many users,

44
00:01:57,800 --> 00:02:00,800
 these data shapes the overall direction of the product.

45
00:02:00,800 --> 00:02:03,120
 Your participation helps improve the experience

46
00:02:03,120 --> 00:02:05,280
 for the wider developer community.

47
00:02:05,280 --> 00:02:08,640
 It's important to know that you have control over telemetry settings.

48
00:02:08,640 --> 00:02:11,800
 You can access these options through the platform's privacy

49
00:02:11,800 --> 00:02:13,400
 and data settings panel.

50
00:02:13,400 --> 00:02:17,120
 From there, you'll find a clear toggle for telemetry collection.

51
00:02:17,120 --> 00:02:20,560
 Turning it off objects you out of sharing usage data.

52
00:02:20,560 --> 00:02:22,160
 A common concern is whether

53
00:02:22,160 --> 00:02:25,280
 disable in telemetry limits functionality and it does not.

54
00:02:25,280 --> 00:02:28,560
 You can continue using all AI features as usual.

55
00:02:28,560 --> 00:02:32,160
 This gives you flexibility based on the type of work you're doing.

56
00:02:32,160 --> 00:02:33,520
 When telemetry is disabled,

57
00:02:33,520 --> 00:02:36,000
 all core features continue to work normally.

58
00:02:36,000 --> 00:02:38,480
 You still have access to AI code generation,

59
00:02:38,480 --> 00:02:40,800
 suggestions and assistant responses.

60
00:02:40,800 --> 00:02:44,640
 Project builds, compilation and real-time help are unaffected.

61
00:02:44,640 --> 00:02:47,920
 The key change is what happens behind the scenes with your data.

62
00:02:47,920 --> 00:02:51,440
 Your usage patterns are no longer stored long-term.

63
00:02:51,440 --> 00:02:55,920
 Your code and prompts won't be used to train future models or guide product planning.

64
00:02:55,920 --> 00:02:59,200
 The important takeaway is that functionality stays the same

65
00:02:59,200 --> 00:03:01,200
 while data retention changes.

66
00:03:01,200 --> 00:03:02,800
 Even with telemetry turned off,

67
00:03:02,800 --> 00:03:05,840
 some temporary cloud processing is still required.

68
00:03:05,840 --> 00:03:10,480
 This is because AI features need cloud resources to generate responses.

69
00:03:10,480 --> 00:03:12,560
 For example, when you submit a prompt,

70
00:03:12,560 --> 00:03:15,200
 it is briefly sent to the cloud for processing.

71
00:03:15,200 --> 00:03:18,880
 The system analyzes the request and returns a result to you.

72
00:03:18,880 --> 00:03:21,920
 After that, the data is discarded rather than stored.

73
00:03:21,920 --> 00:03:25,360
 This process is standard across cloud-based AI tools.

74
00:03:25,360 --> 00:03:30,000
 The key distinction is that your data is not retained once the task is complete.

75
00:03:30,000 --> 00:03:33,440
 Now, when when to enable or disable telemetry is a best practice,

76
00:03:33,440 --> 00:03:37,760
 telemetry can be useful for personal projects learning and open source contributions.

77
00:03:37,760 --> 00:03:41,200
 It's also appropriate for experimentation or non-sensitive code.

78
00:03:41,200 --> 00:03:45,280
 In this case, sharing data helps improve the tool for everyone.

79
00:03:45,280 --> 00:03:50,320
 On the other hand, telemetry should be turned off when working with confidential or proprietary code.

80
00:03:50,320 --> 00:03:52,400
 This includes client work, business logic,

81
00:03:52,400 --> 00:03:54,560
 or anything you wouldn't share publicly.

82
00:03:54,560 --> 00:03:58,160
 Making this choice deliberately helps protect sensitive information.

83
00:03:58,160 --> 00:03:59,840
 See you in the next lesson.

